"""Authored alternate methods, not quotations from the question bank.

Calculator entries use the standard numeric graphing calculator, not a CAS.
Examples are illustrative; replace their numbers with those in the question.
"""
BY_TYPE = {}
def add(key, tool, entry, check):
    BY_TYPE[key] = {'choose': tool, 'entry': entry, 'check': check}

add('le1-solve','Desmos: intersect two expressions','For 5(x-3)+2=3x+7, enter y=5(x-3)+2 and y=3x+7. Select the intersection; its x-coordinate is 10. Then evaluate any requested expression, e.g. 2(10)+1.','Both sides must agree at the reported x. Widen the x-window if the intersection is off screen; the default window cannot establish no solution.')
add('le1-count','Hand: coefficient difference','Reduce to (a-c)x=d-b. If a-c is nonzero there is one solution; if it is zero, compare d-b with zero. For a parameter, first solve a-c=0 and then check the constant difference.','A zero coefficient is a case to inspect before dividing: 0x=0 allows every real x; 0x=7 allows none.')
add('le1-model','Hand: units and boundary case','Make a two-column ledger: fixed amount and amount per item. For a $12 fee plus $4 per item, test the proposed equation at 0 items ($12) and 1 item ($16).','Tests can reject choices but do not generally prove an identity. The equation must also follow the story and use the stated domain.')
add('le1-word','Desmos: graph the modeled total','For a $12 fee plus $4 each totaling $60, enter y=12+4x and y=60. Read x=12 at their intersection.','Label x before entering anything; check nonnegative or integer restrictions and compute the final quantity requested.')
add('lf-eval','Desmos: function notation','Enter f(x)=3x-7, then f(4) for an output. To find an input producing 11, graph y=f(x) and y=11 and select their intersection.','An intersection is an ordered pair: input first, output second. A constant function may have no matching input or every input.')
add('lf-model','Hand: choose a meaningful origin','If a tank has 80 liters at t=3 and loses 5 liters per minute, use V(t)=80-5(t-3). Evaluate V(3) and V(4) before expanding.','The coefficient outside a shifted input is still a rate; the written constant 80 is the amount at t=3, not necessarily at t=0.')
add('lf-interpret','Hand: finite difference','For C(t)=40+25(t-2), compute C(2)=40 and C(3)-C(2)=25. Attach units: dollars at month 2, and dollars per additional month.','A standalone number need not be the initial value when the input is shifted. Read the complete expression and the time origin.')
add('lf-intercept','Desmos: evaluate before graphing','Enter f(x)=2x-6 and f(0). Graph y=f(x) and y=0 to locate the x-intercept (3,0).','For f(x)=0 every x is a zero; for a nonzero constant there is none. Report a coordinate or a single value as requested.')
add('lf-table','Desmos: table regression','Enter the given inputs and outputs in a table with columns x_1 and y_1. Enter y_1~m x_1+b. Read the fitted m and b; verify every listed row.','Exact linear data should fit all rows, apart from numerical roundoff. An approximate regression does not prove exact linearity; repeated x values with different outputs cannot define a function.')
add('lf-graph','Hand: use widely separated exact points','Record two labeled points from the line, with the actual axis units. For (2,7) and (8,19), compute (19-7)/(8-2)=2, then 7-2(2)=3.','More separation reduces reading error. Use exact marked coordinates when supplied; a screenshot does not turn an approximate grid reading into an exact value.')
add('lf-build','Hand: point-slope form','Through (2,7) and (8,19), write y-7=((19-7)/(8-2))(x-2). Expand only if the choices require it.','If the two x-coordinates agree, the line is vertical; it cannot be written as a linear function y=f(x). Identical points alone do not determine a unique line.')
add('le2-parallel','Hand: coefficient vectors','For Ax+By=C, a parallel line has proportional normal vector (A,B). A perpendicular line can use normal vector (-B,A). Example: perpendicular to 3x+4y=12 through (1,2) is -4x+3y=2.','This also handles horizontal and vertical lines. The slope formula -A/B requires B nonzero, and a proper line requires (A,B) not both zero.')
add('le2-model','Hand: extreme feasible cases','For $3 small items and $5 large items totaling $30, use 3s+5l=30. Test the all-small endpoint (10,0) and all-large endpoint (0,6).','Endpoints help detect swapped coefficients. Counts must also be nonnegative integers; the full line includes points the story excludes.')
add('le2-interpret','Hand: solve for one quantity','In 3s+5l=30, solve l=6-(3/5)s. The 5 is dollars per large item; -3/5 is the change in large-item count per extra small item at fixed total.','A coefficient in standard form is not automatically a slope. Distinguish the units of the original coefficient from those of the rearranged rate.')
add('le2-intercept','Hand: axis substitution with degeneracies','For Ax+By=C, test (0,C/B) when B is nonzero and (C/A,0) when A is nonzero. Compute slope -A/B only when B is nonzero.','x=0 is the whole y-axis, so its y-intercepts are not a unique point. A zero or inconsistent equation is not a proper line.')
add('le2-point','Desmos: residuals for a table','Put candidate points in table columns x_1,y_1. For 2x+3y=12, enter 2x_1+3y_1-12. Every residual must be 0 for the entire table to fit.','A single zero residual certifies only that point. Inspect every row and preserve the coordinate order.')
add('le2-solve','Hand: isolate the requested expression','For 4x+2y=18 with x=3, evaluate y=(18-4(3))/2. If the question instead asks 2y, calculate 18-4(3) directly.','Never divide by a coefficient before checking it is nonzero. A zero remaining coefficient gives either no solution or no unique value.')
add('sys-solve','Desmos: equations as written','Enter 3x+2y=19 and x-2y=1 directly. Select (5,2), then evaluate the requested expression, such as 2(5)-2.','Substitute both coordinates into both original equations. Expand the graph window around the expected scales if needed; rounded display coordinates need an exact check.')
add('sys-count','Hand: determinant, then consistency','For a_1 x+b_1 y=c_1 and a_2 x+b_2 y=c_2, calculate D=a_1 b_2-a_2 b_1. D nonzero means one solution. If D=0, eliminate a variable and inspect the remaining constant equation.','Avoid coefficient ratios, which can divide by zero. First handle 0x+0y=0 (no constraint) and 0x+0y=c with c nonzero (impossible).')
add('sys-model','Hand: coefficient unit audit','Write one row per condition and one column per unknown. For 10 tickets at $3 or $5 costing $42, rows are s+l=10 and 3s+5l=42.','Every term in a row must have the same units. Not every system story has a count equation: two mixtures may instead give two mass constraints.')
add('sys-graph','Hand: distinguish coincidence from no visible crossing','Use two labeled points to recover each line when exact values are available. Compare slopes and intercepts algebraically rather than judging nearly parallel pixels.','Parallel distinct lines have no solution; coincident lines have infinitely many. A crossing outside the displayed graph still counts.')
add('sys-word','Hand: subtract a baseline','For 10 tickets, $3 basic and $5 premium, total $42: all-basic cost would be $30. The $12 excess buys $2 upgrades, so there are 6 premium tickets and 4 basic.','This works when the count is fixed and both unit prices are constant. Check both the total count and total value.')
add('inq-model','Hand: test the boundary and nearby values','For at most $50 with a $10 fee and $4 each, write 10+4x<=50. Test x=10, then x=11, then x=0.','Boundary equality must be allowed for at most/at least. A few numerical tests support rejection but the wording establishes the direction.')
add('inq-maxmin','Hand: neighboring integer check','For 10+4x<50, x<10, so the greatest whole number is 9. Test 9 and 10 in the original inequality. For x>10 the least whole number is 11.','Strict bounds require special care when the boundary is an integer. Include nonnegative and any other constraints before rounding.')
add('inq-point','Desmos: direct numerical comparison','For 2x+3y<=12 and candidate (3,2), evaluate 2(3)+3(2) and compare 12<=12. Repeat for every row of a proposed table.','Equality works only for <= or >=. For a system of inequalities, each point must satisfy every inequality.')
add('inq-graph','Desmos: compare shaded half-planes','Enter the candidate inequality exactly, for example y>2x-3. Compare boundary style and a known shaded point from the problem.','The origin is useful only if it is off the boundary and its shading is unambiguous. For vertical boundaries use x<c rather than a slope formula.')
add('eqx-rewrite','Hand plus Desmos: falsify then establish','Define f(x) as the original and g(x) as a choice. Compare f(2),g(2), then another allowed input to eliminate mismatches. Establish the survivor by expansion or factoring.','Agreement at one input does not prove identity. Agreement at d+1 distinct inputs proves equality of polynomials of degree at most d only when that degree bound is established.')
add('eqx-coef','Hand: recover quadratic coefficients from values','If an expression is known to be quadratic f(x)=ax^2+bx+c, calculate c=f(0), b=(f(1)-f(-1))/2, and a=(f(1)+f(-1))/2-c.','This uses the known degree bound; it is not valid for an unrestricted function. Preserve all parameter conditions and check the expanded expression.')
add('eqx-exp','Hand: track the real domain','For positive x, x^(1/2)x^(3/2)=x^2. For unrestricted real x, sqrt(x^2)=abs(x), not x. Use positive nonunit test values to reject wrong choices.','Fractional exponent rules need compatible real domains. Negative bases may make roots undefined in real arithmetic; a calculator decimal is not an algebraic proof.')
add('eqx-rational','Hand: keep an exclusion ledger','For (x^2-9)/(x-3), record x!=3 before factoring to x+3. For sums, multiply each numerator by the missing denominator factors before combining.','Cancellation removes factors, never terms across addition. The simplified expression agrees with the original only on the original domain; its graph may hide a hole.')
add('nle-solve','Desmos: original left and right sides','For x^2-3x=4, graph y=x^2-3x and y=4. Read x=-1 and x=4 at the intersections.','Graphing reports numerical candidates; verify in the original. An empty default window does not prove no roots; use algebra to establish the total count.')
add('nle-sign','Hand: factor sign and ordering','For 2x^2-3x-2=0, factor (2x+1)(x-2)=0, giving -1/2 and 2. Identify positive, negative, greater, or lesser only after listing both.','Zero is neither positive nor negative. Radical and rational equations may reject one candidate on substitution, so filter before ordering.')
add('nle-literal','Hand: isolate with conditions','From V=pi r^2 h, h=V/(pi r^2) requires r!=0. From A=s^2 with s a length, s=sqrt(A), using A>=0 and s>=0.','Division by a parameter may discard a zero case. Squared unknowns can need both signs unless the story imposes nonnegativity; Desmos is not a symbolic equation solver.')
add('nle-count','Hand: discriminant with a degree check','Reduce to ax^2+bx+c=0. For a!=0, D=b^2-4ac gives 2,1,0 distinct real roots as D is positive, zero, negative. Solve the corresponding inequality when a parameter is involved.','If a=0, inspect bx+c=0 separately. For rational or squared equations, excluded or extraneous roots can change the original equation’s count.')
add('nle-sum','Hand: Vieta with root validity','For 3x^2-5x-2=0, sum of the two roots is 5/3 and product is -2/3. If real solutions are required, first check D>=0.','Requires a nonzero quadratic coefficient. These are the roots of that polynomial with multiplicity; excluded roots from a denominator or squaring must be removed before summing original solutions.')
add('nle-system','Desmos plus substitution','Enter y=x^2 and y=2x+3. The intersections are (-1,1) and (3,9). Substitute to get x^2-2x-3=0 if exact coordinates are needed.','A system may have multiple intersections; use algebra to confirm completeness. Do not assume every nonlinear system reduces to a quadratic.')
add('nle-radical','Hand: domain first','For sqrt(x+6)=x, require x>=0 before squaring. Squaring gives (x-3)(x+2)=0; only 3 survives. For fractions, list all denominator zeros first.','Every candidate must satisfy the original equation. Graph the original sides if checking numerically; squaring alone loses sign information.')
add('nlf-eval','Desmos: nested evaluation','Enter f(x)=2(x-3)^2+1. Then enter f(-2), f(1+2), or f(f(0)) exactly as asked, using parentheses around negative inputs.','f(x+2) changes the input and f(x)+2 changes the output. A function may be undefined at a requested input because of roots or denominators.')
add('nlf-vertex','Hand: vertex plus endpoints','For f(x)=-2x^2+8x+1, calculate h=-8/(2(-2))=2 and f(2)=9. On a restricted interval compare this value, if h is in the interval, with endpoint values.','A quadratic requires a!=0. A vertex outside the allowed domain is not the domain’s extremum; an open endpoint may give a bound that is never attained.')
add('nlf-exp','Hand: normalize the time step','Doubling every 3 days from 50 gives P(t)=50*2^(t/3). To convert to a daily multiplier calculate 2^(1/3), then daily percent 100(2^(1/3)-1).','Use positive bases for a continuous real exponential model. The initial amount occurs at t=0 only when the exponent is zero there; do not add successive percentages.')
add('nlf-exp-int','Hand: compare consecutive modeled times','For P(t)=80*1.06^(t/2), calculate P(t+2)/P(t)=1.06. It represents 6% growth per 2 units of time; per-unit growth is 100(sqrt(1.06)-1) percent.','The base’s time period comes from the whole exponent. A constant before a shifted exponent need not be the amount at time zero.')
add('nlf-height','Desmos: physical domain restriction','For h(t)=-16t^2+64t+5, enter y=-16x^2+64x+5 {x>=0}. Locate the vertex and positive ground intersection; evaluate y at the vertex for the height.','Restrict to the modeled time interval, usually from launch until impact. Ground level may be a value other than zero, and the launch itself can be a zero-time root.')
add('nlf-interpret','Hand: use a dimensioned input check','For A(w)=w(2w+3), annotate w as width and 2w+3 as length. At w=2, these give 2 and 7 length units, hence area 14 square units.','A product’s constant is not automatically a starting amount. Interpret the requested factor or coefficient within the entire model and its units.')
add('nlf-zeros','Hand: multiplicity and domain','For f(x)=2(x-1)^2(x+3), zeros are 1 and -3; the even-multiplicity zero at 1 touches the axis without crossing.','The leading factor must be nonzero. A rational function’s canceled factor may be a hole rather than a zero; denominator zeros are excluded.')
add('nlf-yint','Desmos: evaluate a defined function','For f(x)=3*2^(x-1)+4, enter the function then f(0), giving 5.5. For an unknown scale a passing through (2,10), solve 10=a*2^(2-1)+4 by hand.','A graph has a y-intercept only if x=0 is in its domain. The written additive constant need not be the intercept.')
add('nlf-graph','Desmos: table of candidate functions','Enter a candidate f(x), then create a table and put in the exact x-values given by the problem. Compare all corresponding outputs, including signs and turning behavior.','A few matching points do not prove two arbitrary curves identical. Check the stated function family, domain, and all distinguishing information.')
add('nlf-transform','Desmos: define the original once','Enter f(x)=x^2, then g(x)=f(x-3)+2. The original point (0,0) moves to (3,2). Compare a second point, such as (1,1) moving to (4,3).','Changes inside the input act horizontally. For g(x)=f(kx), nonzero k scales horizontal coordinates by 1/k; account for reflection if k<0.')
add('nlf-geom','Hand plus Desmos: restrict feasible lengths','A 10-by-16 sheet with corner squares x cut out has V(x)=x(10-2x)(16-2x), with 0<x<5. Graph y=x(10-2x)(16-2x) {0<x<5}.','Positive x alone is insufficient: every remaining dimension must be positive. A cubic volume model is not a quadratic; compare all feasible candidates.')
add('rat-convert','Hand: unit fractions, then calculator','For 4.76 miles/second to miles/hour, enter 4.76*3600. Write (miles/second)*(3600 seconds/hour) alongside the entry.','Cancel the units before calculating; use the supplied conversion rather than a rounded remembered constant.')
add('rat-square','Hand: raise the entire conversion','For 72 square feet to square yards, enter 72/(3^2)=8. For 2 cubic feet to cubic inches, enter 2*12^3=3456.','Area uses a squared length factor and volume a cubed one; do not square the measured area itself.')
add('rat-ratio','Hand: ratio parts','For a:b=3:5 and total 64, write a=3k,b=5k, so 8k=64 and a=24. For a:b=3:5 with b=40, use 5k=40.','Identify whether the known quantity is one part or the combined total. Ratios require a nonzero denominator.')
add('rat-rate','Hand: weighted distance and time','For 30 miles at 20 mph and 30 miles at 60 mph, enter (30+30)/(30/20+30/60)=30 for the overall mph.','Average speed is total distance divided by total time, including stops when relevant; averaging speeds directly works only for equal time intervals.')
add('rat-density','Hand: recover the occupied space','For a cylinder radius 2 cm and height 5 cm, volume is pi*2^2*5. At density 3 grams/cm^3, enter 3*pi*2^2*5 for mass.','Density units must match the volume units. Distinguish radius from diameter and mass density from population per area.')
add('rat-other','Hand: one-unit expression','If q units cost c dollars at a constant unit price, k units cost k*c/q. Test k=q to recover c and k=0 to recover zero.','Requires q!=0 and direct proportionality. A fixed fee breaks this model; boundary tests do not alone prove the formula.')
add('pct-of','Desmos: solve a numerical proportion','For 24 is p percent of 80, enter 100*24/80=30. For a count, 30% of 80 is entered as (30/100)*80.','Identify the reference whole before dividing. A proportion 0.30 and a percent 30 have different requested forms.')
add('pct-change','Hand: reverse through multipliers','If a value rises 20% then falls 10%, its multiplier is 1.2*0.9=1.08. Final 270 means original 270/(1.2*0.9)=250.','Report net increase as 100(1.08-1)=8 percent. Reversal requires nonzero multipliers; a 100% decrease cannot be uniquely reversed.')
add('1vd-mean','Desmos: weighted list arithmetic','Enter v=[0,1,2,3,4] and f=[2,5,4,3,1], then total(f*v)/total(f). For an ordinary raw list L, use mean(L).','List multiplication is element by element. Frequencies must pair with their values and be nonnegative; total frequency must be positive.')
add('1vd-median','Hand: cumulative positions','For values 0,1,2,3 with frequencies 2,3,4,1, cumulative counts are 2,5,9,10. Positions 5 and 6 hold 1 and 2, so median is 1.5.','Do not use median of the frequency list or unweighted category list. For even n average positions n/2 and n/2+1.')
add('1vd-spread','Desmos: population or sample spread','For raw data enter L=[1,2,2,5], then max(L)-min(L). Use stdevp(L) for population standard deviation or stdev(L) for sample standard deviation, as specified.','Desmos stdev uses n-1; stdevp uses n. Comparing the two formulas across unequal sample sizes can change results. Translation preserves spread; multiplication by k scales standard deviation by abs(k).')
add('1vd-change','Hand: track mean and central positions separately','For n values with mean m, adding z gives (n*m+z)/(n+1); removing z gives (n*m-z)/(n-1). For a median, re-index the sorted data after the edit.','Removing an extreme can change the median substantially when middle gaps are large: removing 1000 from [0,100,1000] changes the median from 100 to 50. The mean change also depends on n.')
add('1vd-read','Hand: recover only what the chart encodes','For a dot plot, transcribe repeated values into a raw list and use mean(L) or median(L). For a box plot, read the median and quartiles directly; IQR=Q3-Q1.','A box plot generally cannot determine the exact mean or sample size. Whiskers may show extrema or a specified outlier convention; use the chart legend.')
add('2vd-predict','Desmos: evaluate the supplied model','If the displayed fitted line is y=2.5x+10, enter f(x)=2.5x+10 then f(8). If only a drawn line is supplied, use two points on that line to estimate its equation.','Do not refit the observed dots when the question asks about the given fit. Label extrapolation outside the observed input range as less supported.')
add('2vd-model','Desmos: compare regressions and residuals','Put data in x_1,y_1. Enter y_1~m x_1+b for linear and, for positive-response growth data, y_1~a*b^(x_1). Compare predictions and residual patterns.','A small residual alone does not establish an appropriate model. Use shape, units, positive exponential base, and the question’s intended family; avoid extrapolating wildly.')
add('2vd-slope','Hand: predicted difference with units','For a fitted model W=1.8t+12, compute W(t+1)-W(t)=1.8. If t is weeks and W is grams, say predicted increase of 1.8 grams per week.','Association is not proof of causation. If t measures decades or hundreds of units, one input unit represents that full scale.')
add('2vd-rate','Desmos: secant calculation','Enter f(x)=x^2+1, then (f(5)-f(2))/(5-2)=7. For a graph use the stated endpoint outputs in the same quotient.','Endpoints must have different x-values. This is the average rate over an interval, not necessarily a tangent slope or a constant rate throughout.')
add('2vd-read','Hand: count against explicit criteria','Draw a small checklist of x range, y range, and inclusive endpoints. Count only observed plotted points meeting every condition, one vertical strip at a time.','Do not count points on a fitted line as observations. Check axes for truncated scales, overlapping observations, and whether boundaries are included.')
add('inf-moe','Hand: compare full intervals','An estimate of 62% with margin 4 percentage points gives [58%,66%]. To assess a claim above 60%, notice this interval includes values on both sides of 60%.','This is uncertainty about the population parameter, not the range of individual responses. Larger random samples reduce expected margin under comparable design and confidence level; not every realized interval must be narrower.')
add('inf-estimate','Hand: sample proportion times eligible population','If 36 of 120 randomly selected residents have a feature, estimate 9000*(36/120)=2700 among 9000 residents.','Use the population from which the sample was selected. This is an estimate, not an exact census; a convenience sample may not support population inference.')
add('stc-general','Hand: trace the sampling frame','Write population -> randomly selected sample -> respondents. If students were randomly selected from one school, identify that school’s students as the potential inference population.','Nonresponse and coverage can still introduce bias. Random assignment to treatments does not by itself make a sample representative of a broader population.')
add('stc-design','Hand: separate selection and assignment','Make two yes/no boxes: random selection supports generalization to the sampled population; random assignment supports a causal treatment comparison under a sound experiment.','An observational association alone does not establish causation. Volunteer sampling may be biased; it does not guarantee a particular direction or magnitude of bias.')
add('av-area','Hand: decompose before calculating','For a 10-by-8 rectangle with a right-triangle notch of legs 3 and 4, enter 10*8-(1/2)*3*4=74. For a circle with diameter 10 use pi*(10/2)^2.','Use perpendicular height for a triangle and square units for area. Subtract only removed, nonoverlapping pieces; preserve exact pi when requested.')
add('av-perimeter','Hand: trace the exterior boundary','For a rectangle width w and length 2w+3, write P=2w+2(2w+3). In a composite figure, trace each exposed boundary segment once.','Shared interior edges are not perimeter. For a removed corner, added notch edges can replace equal lengths; area change alone does not determine perimeter change.')
add('av-volume','Desmos: inverse volume with a positive domain','For a cylinder of radius 3 and volume 90pi, enter 90*pi/(pi*3^2) to find height 10. For sphere radius from volume V, use (3V/(4pi))^(1/3).','Use the correct solid’s factor, and positive physical dimensions. If a diagram gives diameter, halve it before squaring or cubing; keep exact units.')
add('av-surface','Hand: face inventory','For an open-top box length 5, width 3, height 2, enter 5*3+2*5*2+2*3*2=47. List the base and four side faces before adding.','Surface area uses square units. Include only exposed faces named by the question; a cylinder may require zero, one, or two circular ends.')
add('av-scale','Hand: derive length factor first','If similar shapes have area ratio 9:25, their positive length factor is sqrt(25/9)=5/3; volume factor is (5/3)^3=125/27.','Area and volume power rules require uniform similarity. Changing only one dimension uses only that dimension’s factor, not k^2 or k^3.')
add('lat-parallel','Hand: reduce to one acute angle','Mark an angle theta at one crossing. Vertical and corresponding angles copy theta; adjacent straight-line angles become 180-theta. Label each crossing before solving.','Corresponding and alternate angle equalities require parallel lines. Do not infer parallelism from appearance; use the stated facts or a valid converse.')
add('lat-lines','Hand: equations from a local intersection','For adjacent angles 3x+10 and 5x-6 on a straight line, solve (3x+10)+(5x-6)=180, giving x=22. Then compute each angle.','A variable is not necessarily the requested angle. Vertical angles are opposite at the same intersection; adjacent angles are not generally equal.')
add('lat-triangle','Hand: constrain angle values','For a triangle with angles x,2x,3x, solve 6x=180, then list 30,60,90. For a regular n-gon, each interior angle is 180(n-2)/n.','Each triangle angle must be strictly between 0 and 180 degrees. The regular-polygon formula for each angle requires regularity, though the total formula only requires a simple polygon.')
add('lat-similar','Hand: correspondence table','For ABC similar to DEF, write A<->D,B<->E,C<->F and AB/DE=BC/EF=AC/DF. Use one known pair to scale every other length.','Correspondence follows the named order, even under rotation or reflection. Congruence has scale factor 1; angle equality alone does not give equal side lengths.')
add('rtt-pythag','Desmos: difference of squares','If hypotenuse is 13 and one leg is 5, enter sqrt(13^2-5^2)=12. If the two legs are given, use sqrt(a^2+b^2).','Confirm the triangle is right and identify the longest side as hypotenuse. The radicand cannot be negative for a real physical length.')
add('rtt-trig','Desmos: explicit angle units','For a 30-degree angle with hypotenuse 10, enter 10*sin(30*pi/180) in radian mode, or switch to degree mode and enter 10*sin(30).','Do not mix degree inputs with radian settings. Opposite and adjacent depend on the named angle; inverse sine returns an angle, not a side ratio.')
add('rtt-complement','Hand: use the acute-angle restriction','In a right triangle A+B=90 degrees, so sin(A)=cos(B). If cos(B)=3/5, then sin(A)=3/5 without finding either angle.','The inference sin(A)=cos(B) => A+B=90 degrees needs A and B acute; outside that domain periodicity produces other possibilities.')
add('rtt-special','Hand: select a scale variable','For a 30-60-90 triangle with long leg 9, set k*sqrt(3)=9. Then short leg k=3sqrt(3) and hypotenuse 2k=6sqrt(3).','The short leg is opposite 30 degrees, not necessarily the horizontal side. Keep exact radicals when the requested form is exact.')
add('rtt-similar','Hand: altitude-to-hypotenuse products','For a right triangle whose altitude divides the hypotenuse into p and q, h^2=pq; each leg squared equals the full hypotenuse times its adjacent segment.','These identities require the perpendicular altitude to the hypotenuse. Do not apply them to an arbitrary interior segment; match triangles by angle first.')
add('cir-read','Hand: complete squares exactly','For x^2+y^2-3x+5y=2, center is (3/2,-5/2) and radius squared is 2+(3/2)^2+(5/2)^2=21/2. Evaluate sqrt(21/2) only if a decimal is wanted.','Centers need not be integers. First divide by a common nonzero coefficient of x^2 and y^2. Unequal squared coefficients generally do not describe a circle; radius squared must be positive for a nondegenerate real circle.')
add('cir-build','Hand: radius squared from a point','Center (1/2,-2) through (5/2,1) gives r^2=(5/2-1/2)^2+(1+2)^2=13, hence (x-1/2)^2+(y+2)^2=13.','Use squared distance directly: taking a square root then squaring adds rounding. Center signs reverse inside each bracket; coordinate units determine squared equation units.')
add('cir-transform','Hand: transform center and radius separately','A circle centered at (1,-2) with r^2=5 shifted right 3 and scaled to twice the radius has center (4,-2) and r^2=20. Write (x-4)^2+(y+2)^2=20.','A translation of the circle differs from dilation about the origin, which also scales the center. Radius scale k multiplies r^2 by k^2.')
add('cir-radians','Desmos: preserve pi and state the mode','Convert 150 degrees with 150*pi/180=5pi/6. In radian mode a unit-circle point at this angle is (cos(5*pi/6),sin(5*pi/6)).','An angle in radians is dimensionless; do not multiply by pi twice when the supplied angle already contains pi. Decimal calculator values do not replace exact requested multiples of pi.')
add('cir-arc','Hand: one angle fraction for both measures','For radius 6 and central angle 60 degrees, arc length=(60/360)*2*pi*6=2pi and sector area=(60/360)*pi*6^2=6pi. With theta in radians use r*theta and (1/2)*r^2*theta.','Arc is length units; sector is square units. Choose the minor or major angle asked for. The radian formulas cannot take a raw degree number.')
add('cir-tangent','Hand: normal-vector equation','For center (h,k) and tangency point (p,q), write (p-h)(x-p)+(q-k)(y-q)=0. Center (1,2), point (4,2) gives x=4.','First verify the point lies on the circle. The vector formula handles horizontal or vertical radii without division; negative-reciprocal slopes require a defined nonzero radius slope.')
add('cir-geo','Hand: build auxiliary radii','Draw radii to named points on the circle and to each tangency point. Mark equal radii and the right angle between radius and tangent; then use triangle angle sums.','A central angle is twice an inscribed angle subtending the same arc. Choose the correct arc and do not assume an unmarked chord is a diameter or the drawing is to scale.')
